1
00:00:00,215 --> 00:00:03,759
We’ll combine a Gaussian-reconstructed
environment with a volumetric performer in

2
00:00:03,799 --> 00:00:08,010
Unity, then add navigation, proximity activation
and teleportation.

3
00:00:08,648 --> 00:00:11,163
Gsplat renders the static environment.

4
00:00:11,203 --> 00:00:13,972
DynGsplat plays the dynamic sequence.

5
00:00:14,012 --> 00:00:17,173
DualGS provides the human reconstruction and data
workflow.

6
00:00:17,815 --> 00:00:21,334
The first option is to use pretrained Gaussian
PLY models linked in the official

7
00:00:21,374 --> 00:00:22,950
3DGS repository.

8
00:00:23,572 --> 00:00:26,716
The second option is to get a scene from
SuperSplat.

9
00:00:26,756 --> 00:00:28,777
The DREAMS lab used here is available there.

10
00:00:29,415 --> 00:00:34,399
For a SOG scene, open it in the SuperSplat
editor, choose PLY in the export panel,

11
00:00:34,439 --> 00:00:35,900
and turn off Compress PLY.

12
00:00:36,548 --> 00:00:40,517
You can also create your own scene with a phone,
using apps such as Scaniverse,

13
00:00:40,557 --> 00:00:45,251
Polycam or KIRI Engine. Export the reconstructed
Gaussian scene in a compatible

14
00:00:45,291 --> 00:00:46,943
format and import it into Unity.

15
00:00:47,582 --> 00:00:52,731
For clips such as Bass, follow the dataset links
in the DualGS repository.

16
00:00:52,771 --> 00:00:56,745
Reconstruct the multi-view recordings with
DualGS, then compress the appearance

17
00:00:56,785 --> 00:01:01,914
Gaussian PLY sequence with DynGsplat into DGS
metadata and data blocks for Unity.

18
00:01:01,954 --> 00:01:06,338
A prepared DGS sequence from the authors or
course team, when available, can be

19
00:01:06,378 --> 00:01:11,007
imported directly. This demo uses an existing
preprocessed Bass sequence.

20
00:01:11,638 --> 00:01:14,223
Create a 3D project in Unity Hub.

21
00:01:14,263 --> 00:01:16,583
This example uses the Built-in Render Pipeline.

22
00:01:17,215 --> 00:01:22,099
For this desktop setup, select Direct3D 12 as the
graphics API and Gamma as the

23
00:01:22,139 --> 00:01:22,990
color space.

24
00:01:23,658 --> 00:01:28,347
Open Package Manager and install Gsplat first,
followed by DynGsplat.

25
00:01:28,387 --> 00:01:32,476
This example uses existing local packages: choose
Install package from disk and

26
00:01:32,516 --> 00:01:35,383
select each package’s package.json file.

27
00:01:35,995 --> 00:01:41,244
Before importing the dynamic sequence, open
Window, Asset Management, Addressables,

28
00:01:41,284 --> 00:01:45,260
Groups. Create Addressables settings if they have
not been initialized.

29
00:01:45,915 --> 00:01:49,634
Place the Gaussian PLY inside Assets and let
Unity import it.

30
00:01:49,674 --> 00:01:53,838
Create an environment object, add a Gsplat
Renderer, and assign the imported

31
00:01:53,878 --> 00:01:54,850
Gsplat Asset.

32
00:01:55,492 --> 00:01:59,776
Import the complete performer folder, keeping the
DGS metadata and all data blocks

33
00:01:59,816 --> 00:02:04,649
together, outside Resources. Add a DynGsplat
Renderer to the performer object and

34
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:06,087
assign the dynamic asset.

35
00:02:06,715 --> 00:02:10,029
Check the up axis, scale and ground alignment.

36
00:02:10,069 --> 00:02:13,733
Use a sign or another asymmetric landmark to
check for mirroring.

37
00:02:13,773 --> 00:02:16,840
Place the performer’s feet on the floor and
adjust its orientation.

38
00:02:17,482 --> 00:02:19,876
Position the camera at the entrance.

39
00:02:19,916 --> 00:02:23,897
Create markers for the entrance, the studio
viewpoint and the performance center.

40
00:02:24,558 --> 00:02:27,453
Add the tour controller to an empty GameObject.

41
00:02:27,493 --> 00:02:32,156
Assign the camera, environment, performer and
location markers in the Inspector.

42
00:02:32,196 --> 00:02:35,683
Set separate entry and exit distances to avoid
flickering at the boundary.

43
00:02:36,315 --> 00:02:38,629
Movement follows the camera heading.

44
00:02:38,669 --> 00:02:42,548
The proximity check controls the performer’s
visibility and playback.

45
00:02:42,588 --> 00:02:44,780
Teleportation applies the saved viewpoint.

46
00:02:45,415 --> 00:02:50,284
Press Play. We start at the entrance, move
through the environment, and teleport

47
00:02:50,324 --> 00:02:54,180
to the studio. The performer appears when we
enter the viewing range.

48
00:02:54,815 --> 00:02:58,064
Move around the performer while the sequence
keeps playing.

49
00:02:58,104 --> 00:03:00,783
View it from the front, side and rear.

50
00:03:00,823 --> 00:03:04,410
Notice how the person, instrument and background
change with the viewpoint.
